
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The London School of Economics and Political Science (LSE) and the University of Toronto have launched new initiatives to support and focus philosophical research on various aspects of artificial intelligence (AI).</p>



<span id="more-27219"></span>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1024" height="567" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Paolozzi-Eduardo-Perception-through-Impression-The-Conditional-Probability-Machine-1024x567.png" alt="" class="wp-image-27220" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Paolozzi-Eduardo-Perception-through-Impression-The-Conditional-Probability-Machine-1024x567.png 1024w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Paolozzi-Eduardo-Perception-through-Impression-The-Conditional-Probability-Machine-300x166.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Paolozzi-Eduardo-Perception-through-Impression-The-Conditional-Probability-Machine-768x425.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Paolozzi-Eduardo-Perception-through-Impression-The-Conditional-Probability-Machine-250x138.png 250w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Paolozzi-Eduardo-Perception-through-Impression-The-Conditional-Probability-Machine.png 1236w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Eduardo Paolozzi, &#8220;Perception through Impressions&#8221; from The Conditional Probability Machine</figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The <a href="https://www.lse.ac.uk/philosophy" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Department of Philosophy, Logic and Scientific Method</a> at LSE has received a $5.3 million donation to launch a new research group&#8212;The <a href="https://www.lse.ac.uk/global-priorities" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><u>Global Priorities Research Group</u></a> (GPRG)&#8212;dedicated to &#8220;determining which problems matter most and what should be done in trying to realize the best outcomes for humanity and the wider world.&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It&#8217;s primary emphasis will be &#8220;philosophical research that helps us navigate the largest-scale challenges associated with the impacts of transformative AI.&#8221; </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Funded by <a href="https://goodventures.org/">Good Ventures</a>, a philanthropic foundation, the GPRG &#8220;will also consider how we should compare and weight the interests of different kinds of beings&#8212;be they human, animal, or perhaps even artificial&#8212;as well as pursuing foundational research on issues related to value and rational choice with the potential to reorient our priorities across a wide range of issues.&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The group will be led by philosophers <a href="https://www.andreasmogensen.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Andreas L. Mogensen</a>, who recently joined LSE from the University of Oxford, and <a href="https://users.ox.ac.uk/~mert2060/">Teruji Thomas</a> (Oxford, LSE), and will be based at LSE&#8217;s <a href="https://www.lse.ac.uk/cpnss" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><u>Centre for Philosophy of Natural and Social Science</u></a>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Meanwhile, the <a href="https://artificialconsciousness.ca/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Artificial Consciousness Initiative</a> is a new project from the <a href="https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">University of Toronto Department of Philosophy</a> that &#8220;aims to advance foundational research connecting artificial intelligence and consciousness.&#8221;</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The project&#8217;s director, philosopher <a href="http://andrewyuanlee.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Andrew Y. Lee</a>, writes:</p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow">
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>The initiative&#8217;s research agenda focuses on questions about the nature, structure, and indicators of artificial consciousness; the valence and character of potential artificial experiences; the welfare and moral status of artificial systems; and the ethical and safety risks raised by increasingly capable AI. The initiative does not presuppose that current artificial systems are conscious or that future ones will be. But it starts from the view that these questions are worth taking seriously.</em></p>
</blockquote>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The core team of the Artificial Consciousness Initiative includes <a href="http://kkvd.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Karina Vold</a>, <a href="http://christiantarsney.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Christian Tarsney</a>, and <a href="https://bradford.rice.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gwen Bradford</a>. </p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The initiative&#8217;s aims include basic research, partnerships with other institutions, and contributing to public and policy discussions related to AI consciousness. The project is supported by a large grant from <a href="https://www.longview.org/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">Longview Philanthropy</a>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Both initiatives are currently hiring postdocs.<br></p>
